  "articleBody": "Thinking about getting new kitchen cabinets installed? It's a bigger job than just screwing things to a wall, believe it or not! Typically, the process kicks off with a good inspection of your new cabinets and the kitchen space itself. Installers will check for any high spots on your floor and locate the wall studs — super important for a secure fit. Then, they'll mark a level reference line, which guides the whole installation. They usually start with an upper corner cabinet and work their way out, clamping adjoining cabinets together as they go. It's all about shimming things just right to make sure everything's level and plumb before fastening the cabinets to the studs. The timing can really depend on the size of your kitchen and how tricky the site conditions are. If your walls or floors aren't perfectly level, or if you have a really long run of cabinets, it might take a bit longer. Also, if you’re changing your kitchen layout significantly, that adds time. Just remember, upper cabinets generally go in before the base cabinets. If you're dealing with uneven surfaces or a complex layout, consider bringing in a professional to make sure it's done right.",
  "datePublished": "2026-08-09"
